---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import boards from "../data/boards.json";
import photos from "../data/board-images.json";
import BoardPhoto from "../components/BoardPhoto.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<Site
  title="Choose a microcontroller board"
  description="Recognise the Pico and XIAO boards, find the right model and check firmware availability before starting your lighting project."
>
  <section class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">THE COMPUTER IN YOUR PROJECT</p>
    <h1>Choose your board.</h1>
    <p class="intro">The small computer at the heart of your project.</p>
    <p>
      A microcontroller has a processor to run instructions and memory to hold
      programs and values. A development board adds a USB socket and pins so you
      can connect it to other parts. With LightVM installed, it can run a
      lighting pattern on its own while receiving commands from your computer.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose" id="find-a-board">
    <h2>Where do I find one?</h2>
    <p>
      For a first lighting project, the <strong>Raspberry Pi Pico 2 W</strong>{" "}
      is the example used in our{" "}
      <a href={`${base}build/`}>parts and build guide</a>. Start at{" "}
      <a href="https://www.raspberrypi.com/products/raspberry-pi-pico-2/">
        Raspberry Pi’s product page and retailer finder
      </a>
      , select <strong>Pico 2 W</strong>, and choose your country. Look for a
      version with headers already soldered on if you want to use jumper wires.
      A Pico is a microcontroller board, not a Raspberry Pi desktop computer;
      this project does not need an SD card, keyboard or monitor for the board.
    </p>
    <p>
      Already have a board? Compare the printed name with the models below. For
      the smaller XIAO boards, use Seeed’s official model guides:{" "}
      <a href="https://wiki.seeedstudio.com/XIAO_ESP32C3_Getting_Started/">
        ESP32-C3
      </a>
      ,{" "}
      <a href="https://wiki.seeedstudio.com/xiao_esp32s3_getting_started/">
        ESP32-S3
      </a>{" "}
      and{" "}
      <a href="https://wiki.seeedstudio.com/xiao_esp32c6_getting_started/">
        ESP32-C6
      </a>
      . Those guides include pin diagrams and purchase links. Check the full
      model name: these ESP32 boards use different firmware.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Before buying:</strong> check the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}releases/`}>release catalogue</a> for your exact model.
      We develop for the boards below, but some may not yet have an installer
      ready to use. Without one, setup requires building the firmware with
      developer tools. You can try the{" "}
      <a href="https://kajlights.github.io/lightvm/">browser playground</a>{" "}
      without buying anything.
    </p>
    <h2>Identify your model</h2>
    <p>
      Use the printed model to identify your board. Flash memory stores software
      and saved work when power is off. The sizes below are the standard sizes
      for these models.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="wrap board-grid" aria-label="Board models">
    {boards.map((board) => (
      <article class="board-card" id={board.id}>
        <BoardPhoto id={board.id as keyof typeof photos} name={board.name} />
        <p class="eyebrow">
          {board.chip} · {board.flash_bytes / 1048576} MiB FLASH STORAGE
        </p>
        <h2>{board.name}</h2>
        <p>{photos[board.id as keyof typeof photos].note}</p>
        <p>{board.hint}</p>
        <p class="muted">
          {board.method === "uf2"
            ? `Hold BOOTSEL while connecting. Copy the selected UF2 to the ${board.drive} drive.`
            : "Choose its USB port in the workspace. Chip, revision and flash must match before installation."}
        </p>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href={`${base}studio/#/firmware?board=${board.id}`}
        >
          Find firmware →
        </a>
      </article>
    ))}
  </section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="image-credit">
      Photos: Raspberry Pi and Seeed Studio. Enlarged, not to the same scale.{" "}
      <a href={`${base}image-credits/`}>Image sources and licences</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
  <section class="wrap section narrow prose">
    <h2>What can your board do?</h2>
    <p>
      A Pico 2 W contains a Wi-Fi radio. To control it over the network, its
      firmware also needs Wi-Fi and network-control features enabled. The
      original Pico has no radio and uses USB. Connect in{" "}
      <a href={`${base}studio/#/devices`}>My devices</a> to read the features
      installed on your board, then try the{" "}
      <a href={`${base}learn/#communications`}>communications activity</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Working with sound</h2>
    <p>
      The XIAO ESP32-S3 Sense adds a microphone. The companion kaj-fi project
      uses it to measure sound and send those measurements to other devices, for
      experiments such as lights that respond to sound level. Public kaj-fi
      firmware downloads are not available yet.
    </p>
  </section>
</Site>
